We keep the 215 K mW liquid frames in a Hugging Face dataset repository.
DVC is the other home for those frames, once a remote is configured.
A dataset repository of that kind is the destination for the dumps.
Zenodo by itself is not that home.

The deck in input/mw215 melts an mW lattice at 500 K for 30 ps.
The velocity seed in that deck is 215.
The same run then stays at 215 K for 50 ps.
The number density is 0.033 molecules per cubic angstrom.
One edge of eight cells gives 512 molecules.
An edge of sixteen cells gives 4096 molecules.
An edge of thirty-two cells gives 32768 molecules.

input/mw215/regenerate.py writes those dumps under data/mw215.
The script sets one OpenMP thread so the seed stays fixed.
It then compares the published CHILL+ rule and ovito-chill+ with OVITO.
We can add a fetch script when the Hugging Face dataset repository exists.
Until then the regenerate script is the way to obtain the frames.
Git keeps the deck and the script.
Git leaves the dumps themselves out of the history.
